<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">William Lovett</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1800">1800</a>–<a href="/years/1877">1877</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/19th-century">19th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">William Lovett was a British activist and a leader of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/chartism">Chartist movement</a>, one of the leading London-based artisan radicals of his generation. Born in Newlyn in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cornwall">Cornwall</a>, he worked in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/london">London</a> as a cabinet maker, helped form the National Union of the Working Classes in 1831 and in June 1836 founded the London Working Men's Association. In 1838 he and Francis Place drafted the parliamentary bill that was the foundation of the People's Charter. Elected secretary of the first Chartist Convention in 1839, he was convicted of seditious libel over placards condemning the police and held in Warwick Gaol until July 1840. A moral-force Chartist who rejected violence, he then turned to education.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/william-lovett.jpg" alt="William Lovett" width="330" height="436" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Contemporary portrait · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:WilliamLovettYoung.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>In 1836 <b>William Lovett</b> and Henry Hetherington founded the London Working Men’s Association, and in the autumn of that year the Carmarthen Working Men’s Association began Chartism in Wales.</blockquote><a href="/movement/chartism">Chartism · Background</a></li><li><blockquote><b>William Lovett</b>, a cabinetmaker, helped draft the People’s Charter, and Feargus O’Connor, an Irish orator who owned the Northern Star newspaper, became the most popular Chartist leader.</blockquote><a href="/movement/labour-movement">Labour movement · People</a></li></ul></section>
